From 099a127a94c537ed91e9361c099601246bdcd7ee Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cruz <cruz@tbu.net>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 18:32:44 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 063/206] test(shorebird): execute IndirectStaticCallInstr for
 the first time

The instruction landed with its nineteen method names matching theirs
exactly, and nothing ever constructed it. Symbol-set parity proves the
shape was transcribed correctly and says nothing about whether any of it
runs, which is the same gap AllocateMetadata has been sitting in.

Five tests, each with a negative control run before committing:

  TypeFromSignatureOnly        type comes from the declared return type
  ConstantPropagatorDoesNotFold  the call is never folded
  PrintOperandsFormat          their format, including the no-argument case
  ArgumentsSizeAndTypeArgs     argument words, and the type-args slot
  SerializationRoundTrip       dd_slot and target survive the IL serializer

Three of these pin judgment calls a reader cannot recover from their
stripped binary -- not folding, not narrowing from the target's body, and
a format that ends at `]` where upstream's ends in a space. Each would
otherwise be silently "fixed" back to the StaticCallInstr behaviour.

Negative controls, two rounds: breaking constant propagation, the format
string, ComputeType and ArgumentsSize turned exactly those four red and
left the fifth green; storing dd_slot+1 turned the round-trip red and left
ArgumentsSize green. Without that the tests only prove they can pass.

The round-trip test is the one that needs a real function from a script:
WriteTrait<const Function&> encodes a target as (owner cid, index within
owner), and a synthetic Function::New is not in its owner's function list.

81 of 81 shorebird tests pass.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

